[2026-08-22] Body extraction: quotequail misses Lotus Notes quote idiom
- Symptom: quotequail correctly splits on "-----Original Message-----" (Outlook)
  but does NOT split on the Lotus Notes idiom "Name <email> on MM/DD/YYYY HH:MM:SS AM/PM"
  followed by To:/cc:/Subject: lines with no ">" quote markers. Confirmed on
  skilling-j/sent_items/39. — an entire inline-quoted message from Kevin Compton
  was left inside the "EXPAND" (authored) chunk.
- Root cause: quotequail's pattern set (_patterns.py) targets Outlook/Gmail/etc.
  reply idioms; Enron mailboxes are heavily Lotus Notes / cc:Mail in origin
  (X-Folder headers reference "Notes Folders"), a client quotequail doesn't model.
- Fix: added a supplementary regex pass in extract.py for the Lotus Notes
  "<name> on <date>" + To:/cc:/Subject: idiom, plus a "Forwarded by ... on <date>"
  idiom. Take the EARLIEST cut point between quotequail's first quoted-chunk
  start and the regex match, use that as the authored/quoted boundary.
- Status: mitigated, not solved. This is a heuristic, not a validated parser.
  Per docs/prior-work-survey.md §3.1/§7: 300-500 bodies must be hand-annotated
  and extraction precision/recall reported before any results are trusted.
- Tags: extraction, quotequail, lotus-notes, enron-corpus

[2026-08-22] analyze.py throughput and CPU contention on shared machine
- Symptom: full-corpus spaCy analysis estimated at ~3.7h based on an initial
  20k-email run (39.4 docs/sec, n_process=4, CPU). A follow-up attempt to
  speed this up (exclude spacy's 'parser' component, more processes) showed
  NO improvement and a subsequent benchmark measured CPU throughput had
  dropped to 14.8 docs/sec.
- Root cause: this machine runs multiple concurrent Claude Code sessions.
  `top` showed load average 17-23 with a single non-pipeline process pegged
  at ~26 CPU-cores. CPU throughput for this pipeline is NOT stable and
  depends on what else is running on the machine at the time.
- Also: excluding spaCy's 'parser' component is safe (verified pos_ tags are
  produced by 'tagger'+'attribute_ruler', not 'parser') and is kept as a
  real optimisation, but multiprocessing (n_process>1) does not help for
  short Enron message bodies -- IPC/pickling overhead dominates compute for
  small docs.
- Fix: added GPU support to analyze.py (--device gpu). GPU is idle on this
  machine and unaffected by other sessions' CPU contention. Constraint: the
  GPU (RTX 3500 Ada, 12GB) is shared with a running llama-server using ~9GB,
  leaving ~3GB free -- cupy OOM'd at batch_size=500. Fixed with
  cupy.get_default_memory_pool().set_limit(size=2*1024**3) and batch_size=64
  (measured best of 32/64/128). GPU n_process is forced to 1 (multiprocessing
  is not supported with a single shared CUDA context).
- Verified GPU output is IDENTICAL to the earlier CPU output on the same
  20,439-email smoke test (367,902 feature rows, same sample values) --
  this is a performance fix only, not a correctness change.
- Status: resolved for now. Full 517k-email run should default to
  --device gpu given ongoing CPU contention; if the GPU is later needed by
  another process, fall back to --device cpu (n_process=1, since
  multiprocessing gave no benefit) and expect a multi-hour run.
- Tags: performance, spacy, gpu, cupy, cpu-contention, multi-session

[2026-08-22] GPU memory freed (killed llama-server) -- throughput unchanged
- Action: user directed killing the llama-server process (PID 5583,
  gemma4-coder-q4_k_m.gguf, ~9GB VRAM) to free GPU memory for this pipeline.
  Confirmed killed; GPU free memory went from ~3GB to ~11.6GB.
- Re-benchmarked GPU batch sizes with the freed memory: batch=128 -> 22.2
  docs/sec, 256 -> 24.9, 512 -> 30.0, 1024 -> 27.5 docs/sec.
- Finding: essentially NO improvement over the earlier VRAM-constrained
  batch=64 result (29.6 docs/sec). Best-of-run (batch=512, 30.0/sec) is
  within noise of the earlier constrained measurement.
- Conclusion: the bottleneck is NOT GPU VRAM or batch size. `top` showed
  load average climbing to 38.5 (up from 17-23 earlier) during this
  benchmark -- CPU contention from other concurrent sessions on this shared
  machine is the actual limiting factor, since spaCy tokenization and
  Python-level doc/orchestration overhead run on CPU regardless of which
  device runs the neural net forward pass. Freeing GPU memory does not
  address a CPU-bound bottleneck.
- Fix: set batch_size=512 as the default for --device gpu (marginal best
  measured value). Did not pursue further tuning -- throughput on this
  machine is bounded by external load that varies independently of any
  pipeline configuration choice.
- Practical implication for the full 517k-email run: expect ~25-30 docs/sec
  regardless of device choice while other sessions are active on this
  machine; ETA is therefore ~4.8-5.7 hours, and this figure will not
  improve materially from further GPU/batch tuning.
- Tags: performance, gpu, cupy, cpu-contention, batch-size

[2026-08-22] Content dedup surfaces a signature-stripping gap; fixed
- Context: built dedupe.py to find different Message-IDs whose authored_text
  (quotes/forwards already stripped) is identical after normalisation, per
  user request. Ran on the 20,439-email smoke test.
- Finding 1 (bug): the largest signature-driven duplicate group was
  "Sue Mara / Enron Corp. / Tel: (415) 782-7802 / Fax:(415) 782-7854" --
  a bare contact-block signature with no closing word ("Thanks,"/"Regards,")
  to trigger the existing _SIGNOFF_RE heuristic in extract.py. This directly
  violated the requirement to ignore signatures when assessing duplicates.
- Fix: added a second signature heuristic to extract.py -- inspect the last
  blank-line-separated block of the authored text; if it's <=6 lines and
  contains a Tel:/Fax:/Phone:/Cell:/Mobile: label or a phone-number pattern,
  strip it regardless of whether a closing word introduced it. Bumped
  EXTRACT_VERSION v1->v2 and FEATURE_VERSION v1-placeholder->v2-placeholder
  so stage_status correctly re-queued all 20,439 emails for reprocessing
  under the new version (this is exactly the scenario the (stage, version)
  keying was designed for).
- Verified: the Sue Mara message's authored_text is now '' (0 chars) --
  correct, since that email's entire body was the signature.
- Effect on dedup: groups 4593->4550, in-group emails 12013->11896 after
  the fix. Smaller than expected because most of the biggest duplicate
  groups turned out to be automated bulk notifications (recurring reports,
  subscription notices), not human signatures -- see Finding 2.
- Finding 2 (not a bug, a corpus characteristic): ~70% of authored-text-
  bearing smoke-test emails fall into SOME duplicate group. Inspecting the
  largest groups shows most are automated recurring content sent to a
  human-looking From address (e.g. a daily "EnronOnline Executive Summary",
  a "Kudlow Commentary" subscription notice, an "Investor Relations" report
  distribution) -- not the ingest.py AUTOMATED_TOKENS address-based filter's
  target (that filter checks the local-part of the address, e.g.
  "noreply@", not the content). This is real duplication by the literal
  definition, but is a different phenomenon from human copy-paste, and
  should probably be excluded from per-person language statistics on a
  different basis (content pattern or high per-sender duplication rate)
  than the current address-based is_automated flag. Not fixed -- flagged
  for a future decision on how automated-content senders should be treated
  in aggregate.py.
- Second bug found in the same session: after freeing GPU VRAM, the
  --device gpu default batch_size (512, set in the earlier 2026-08-22 entry)
  OOM'd against the OLD hardcoded 2GB cupy pool cap, which had been sized
  for the 3GB-free constrained environment before the llama-server was
  killed. Fixed by querying actual free VRAM at runtime
  (cupy.cuda.runtime.memGetInfo()) and setting the pool limit to 70% of
  free, rather than any fixed constant -- robust to whatever else is using
  the GPU at run time. Crash was clean (occurred before any stage_status
  commit); no partial/corrupt state to clean up.
- Tags: extraction, signature-stripping, dedup, gpu, cupy, oom
